; Regression: the IMPLICIT tail return through a BLOCK is judged too
; (2026-08-26, closing a hole in report_null_into_ref#).
;
; That check documents four positions -- a typed binding, a `set`, an explicit
; `return` and the implicit tail return -- and it was judging only the SHALLOW
; tail. `plan_action#` reads its tail flag ONCE, for the body ACTION, so when
; the body is a Block the `null` that actually reaches `ret` is the block's
; LAST STATEMENT and the direct call never saw it.
;
; The two spellings measured one keyword apart, before the fix:
;
;     function nb(b: ref[Box]) returns ref[Box]   null          ->  rc 4
;     function nb(b: ref[Box]) returns ref[Box] { ... null }    ->  rc 0
;
; A closed limit reads exactly like an open one, which is why this stood: the
; check's own comment claimed the position it was not covering. It was live --
; dazzle's `Interpreter.lookup_char_entry` returned a declared non-optional
; `ref[CharPart]` from a body whose tail is literally `null`.
;
; The fix rides check_tail_ref_escape#, the read-only visitor that already
; recurses over variant payloads, choose/if/match arms and a block's last
; statement, and already runs on the implicit tail -- the same traversal
; convert_tail_literal# exists for, and for the same reason.
;
; The positive side is null_into_ref_ok.scaly, whose `walk`/`first_or_null`
; return the nullable form from block bodies and must keep compiling.
;
; xfail: null assigned to a non-optional reference

define Box (v: int)

function find_it(b: ref[Box], want: int) returns ref[Box]
{
    if b.v = want
        return b
    null
}

var b Box(7)
let r find_it(&b, 7)
Console.println "unreachable"
